to another.

Very useful, too.

> Is the Crawl endgame (and are the classes) very much more
> differentiated compared to Nethack?

More classes, more races, more variety. But it depends on how you play.  
You can make that varied, by continuing with some resitances weak, or  
hang around spending ages in varius ways to improve your character,  
ending up with uniform endgame folks, too.[*] Really like Nethack in  
that regard, only executed differently (other means to waste time to get  
a different set of 'AK').

The difference is that you can still get easily killed, no matter how  
much time you waste to prepare. Plodding through the bottom level is  
exciting, rather than a chore.

And there are some branches that you can choose to go to (Hell,  
Pandemonium, Abyss) to get extra excitement and objects (and deaths, of  
course).

You've got more choice in what you do with your character, the startup  
combination doesn't direct a course. If you don't like your god, convert  
to another one, and in fact, some (IIRC most) characters start without  
one. (On the other hand it suffers from random deaths.)

Personally, I like the skill system better.

[*] Take friendly monsters. If you take the time to work up the  
necessary skills for the spell to summon them, you can run around  
letting an army of tentacled mostrosities fight for you (you get half XP  
for what they kill). Or mutations (unlike polymorph in Nethack, they  
give you 1 to 3 intrinsics, which may be great or a real pain). You can  
hang around for ages (on a level that spawns big monsters, so you can  
kill and eat them) mutating yourself until you've got a neat list of  
intrinsics. Then you go back to your stash with all the random artifacts  
(which can be any wearable or wieldable object, they have 1 to 5 or 6 or  
so traits) to complement that.

There's a prefered course, like an Earth Elementalist doing the big  
Earth spells, but there's nothing stopping him from going the Summoner  
path, or another spellcaster doing the nifty Earth spells. The one big  
difference is between fighter types and spellcaster types. Fighters have  
a tough life, because even high level it's no good for them to try  
spells. (No skill, no Int, and everything that makes them tough  
interfering with spells.)

But down at the kit level, you still end up with the same set, whether  
that's one ring of foo restistance in Nethack, or one to three objects  
that give you one or two levels of foo resistance in Crawl. It's just  
that you've got more choice in the latter. (I like that, I just don't  
like the developers, and the bugs.)

But saying Nethack should become like Crawl doesn't work out. They're  
two different games. Some features (like makros and dump) are uniformly  
good, IMO, but gameplay problems in Nethack would be better served  
solving them Nethack-specific. Those who don't like it can play Crawl  
and shut up.

